The trend in the measured values of the spin frequency and orbital period for the AMSPs is shown in Fig. 1. Also shown for comparison are values for the known (rotation-powered) millisecond radio pulsars from the ATNF Pulsar Catalogue4(Manchester et al., 2005); these rotation-powered pulsars are older and thought to be the descendants of the AMSPs. While the rotation-powered pulsars are distributed fairly uniformly in the diagram of \\nu_{\\rm s} versus P_{\\rm orb}, the AMSPs are not. In particular, at the highest rotation rates, the orbital periods of the AMSPs span the range from about 0.5 hr to 20 hr, but there are no AMSPs with 100 Hz \\lesssim\\nu_{\\rm s}<440 Hz and P_{\\rm orb}>4.3 hr. A similar plot to Fig. 1 was presented by Kaaret et al. (2006), who remarked upon a possible absence of slowly-rotating, long orbital period systems; they suggested that this may be due to an accretion rate that varies with orbital period but made no further discussion. A full population synthesis of neutron star spin and orbital periods is beyond the scope of this work. Nevertheless, a qualitative explanation of the \\nu_{\\rm s}-P_{\\rm orb} distribution of AMSPs can be obtained by invoking simple physical processes that occur in their evolution.

