m_{\\rm c}=0.23\\,\\hat{m}_{\\rm c}(P_{\\rm orb}/2\\ {\\rm hr}),  where \\hat{m}_{\\rm c}\\equiv m_{\\rm c}/m_{\\rm c}^{\\rm MS} and m_{\\rm c}^{\\rm MS} is the mass of a main-sequence star that just fills its Roche lobe at P_{\\rm orb}; the factor of \\hat{m}_{\\rm c} (<1) is due to the fact that the companion star in a binary has a larger radius for its mass than an isolated star (see, e.g., Sills et al., 2000; Andronov et al., 2003) and encapsulates our uncertainty in the (less evolved, since we only consider m_{\\rm c}<m_{\\rm x}) evolutionary state of the companion.

We now examine the various physical processes that would lead to a change in the angular momentum of the orbit or neutron star spin and thus cause a source to move within the \\nu_{\\rm s}-P_{\\rm orb}-plane of Fig. 1. Mass transfer from the companion to the neutron star can cause an increase in the size of the orbit, while orbital angular momentum loss due to magnetic braking or gravitational wave emission causes the orbit to decrease. The orbital period which separates expansion and decay is estimated to be >0.5 days (see, e.g., Podsiadlowski et al., 2002; Ma & Li, 2009). For orbital periods longer than one hour, magnetic braking is dominant. We consider two prescriptions for the magnetic braking torque. The first one, from Verbunt & Zwaan (1981), results in a timescale for decay

\\tau_{\\rm decay}^{\\rm VZ}\\approx(2.1\\times 10^{8}\\ {\\rm yr})\\,m_{\\rm x}^{2/3} \\hat{m}_{\\rm c}^{-4/3}(P_{\\rm orb}/2\\ {\\rm hr})^{-2/3}  and a mass transfer rate \\dot{M}_{-11} (in units of 10^{-11}M_{\\odot}\\ {\\rm yr}^{-1}) (see also King et al., 1996)

\\dot{M}_{-11}^{\\rm VZ}=80\\,m_{\\rm x}^{-2/3}\\hat{m}_{\\rm c}^{7/3}(P_{\\rm orb}/2 \\ {\\rm hr})^{5/3}. 

